WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.502 --> 00:00:03.920
That's just a taste.

00:00:03.920 --> 00:00:06.506
In many cases, it is an
inside job, you know.

00:00:06.506 --> 00:00:08.508
There is somebody in a museum,

00:00:08.508 --> 00:00:11.803
who knows about what's
going to take place.

00:00:11.803 --> 00:00:13.471
Hi, my name is Octave Durham,

00:00:13.471 --> 00:00:14.848
nickname Oky.

00:00:14.848 --> 00:00:17.017
I did more than 10,000 burglaries.

00:00:17.017 --> 00:00:18.810
I robbed everything you can imagine,

00:00:18.810 --> 00:00:20.854
from museums, banks, armored trucks,

00:00:20.854 --> 00:00:22.605
but I never used any violence.

00:00:22.605 --> 00:00:23.648
My name is Arthur Brand.

00:00:23.648 --> 00:00:26.568
I'm an art detective, and I'm responsible

00:00:26.568 --> 00:00:29.863
for recovering $250 million of stolen art.

00:00:29.863 --> 00:00:33.450
We now work together in
trying to prevent art heists.

00:00:33.450 --> 00:00:36.077
Today, we're going to
watch art heist clips

00:00:36.077 --> 00:00:38.163
from Hollywood movies,
and we're going to judge

00:00:38.163 --> 00:00:40.248
how realistic they are.

00:00:46.755 --> 00:00:48.798
I know of one museum who has lasers.

00:00:48.798 --> 00:00:51.092
It's the Kunsthistorisches
Museum in Vienna,

00:00:51.092 --> 00:00:54.095
but I don't think it's
like this, you know.

00:00:54.095 --> 00:00:56.848
He can dance around these lasers.

00:00:56.848 --> 00:00:59.100
I'm like, what is this capoeira stuff?

00:00:59.100 --> 00:01:00.018
Are you nuts?

00:01:00.018 --> 00:01:01.978
This does not exist.

00:01:01.978 --> 00:01:04.314
Even if it would exist like this,

00:01:05.440 --> 00:01:09.611
not touching any one of
these lasers is impossible.

00:01:09.611 --> 00:01:10.737
It's impossible.

00:01:10.737 --> 00:01:12.614
Even if you would have seen
them, if you would see them,

00:01:12.614 --> 00:01:15.575
you know, without using
any spray or something,

00:01:15.575 --> 00:01:16.409
it's impossible.

00:01:16.409 --> 00:01:18.495
Arthur: But look at this.

00:01:22.665 --> 00:01:24.918
The practice to do this little dance,

00:01:24.918 --> 00:01:26.544
it will take you about a year.

00:01:26.544 --> 00:01:28.838
The whole collection is gone.

00:01:28.838 --> 00:01:30.465
What you see now is gone in a year.

00:01:30.465 --> 00:01:32.175
You'll be dancing still with your lasers.

00:01:32.175 --> 00:01:35.261
I never studied the alarm for a long time.

00:01:35.261 --> 00:01:36.763
I mean, when you see a building,

00:01:36.763 --> 00:01:38.389
you see how I'm going get in,

00:01:38.389 --> 00:01:41.768
and I see the system, and it will be,

00:01:41.768 --> 00:01:43.978
yeah, studied in a minute.

00:01:43.978 --> 00:01:46.648
It's a system that I can
do, I can hack or not.

00:01:46.648 --> 00:01:48.483
There must've been a
lot of sensors, right?

00:01:48.483 --> 00:01:50.902
I think at least 20 or something.

00:01:50.902 --> 00:01:53.613
It's always one or two or three.

00:01:53.613 --> 00:01:56.032
Two in a corner detect the whole area.

00:01:56.032 --> 00:01:57.909
I really doubt if technology

00:01:57.909 --> 00:02:00.829
will ever be that perfect to prevent

00:02:00.829 --> 00:02:03.164
art thefts from happening,
because you know,

00:02:03.164 --> 00:02:05.792
there is always a button
you can switch off.

00:02:05.792 --> 00:02:08.378
And even if they can't find a button,

00:02:08.378 --> 00:02:10.755
you know, they come in with a gun.

00:02:10.755 --> 00:02:13.299
The average burglar would not do this,

00:02:13.299 --> 00:02:14.926
because he doesn't have the skills

00:02:14.926 --> 00:02:19.430
or the balls to do it,
because it looks very scary.

00:02:19.430 --> 00:02:21.057
They're scared of jail.

00:02:21.057 --> 00:02:23.393
If you can't do the
time, don't do the crime.

00:02:23.393 --> 00:02:25.353
I did crimes, I can do the time.

00:02:25.353 --> 00:02:27.021
I did a lot of time.

00:02:29.691 --> 00:02:31.151
I give this movie a 10.

00:02:32.652 --> 00:02:34.487
No, kidding, a zero.

00:02:34.487 --> 00:02:37.490
The impressionist gallery,
closed for cleaning.

00:02:37.490 --> 00:02:38.366
- Cleaning?

00:02:38.366 --> 00:02:39.242
Yeah.

00:02:39.242 --> 00:02:40.410
Doing it right now.

00:02:40.410 --> 00:02:42.370
You never talk to a guard,

00:02:42.370 --> 00:02:44.289
otherwise he can remember your face.

00:02:44.289 --> 00:02:47.125
You always avoid to see a guard.

00:02:47.125 --> 00:02:50.753
Upstairs sent us down
to clean this exhibit.

00:02:55.800 --> 00:02:57.844
These are the fake guards.

00:02:57.844 --> 00:03:00.305
It's impossible to be in a museum.

00:03:00.305 --> 00:03:03.057
And to me, this guy is too heavily guard.

00:03:03.057 --> 00:03:05.810
He has like a Taser, and it's impossible.

00:03:05.810 --> 00:03:09.314
In the Netherlands, it's not even allowed.

00:03:15.653 --> 00:03:17.906
I've never seen these big kinds.

00:03:17.906 --> 00:03:19.199
Probably, they exist somewhere.

00:03:19.199 --> 00:03:20.241
Maybe in the Louvre.

00:03:20.241 --> 00:03:21.993
When it happens, when the alarm goes off,

00:03:21.993 --> 00:03:25.288
it goes down, and they use something.

00:03:25.288 --> 00:03:28.416
It could be like that, but in a museum,

00:03:28.416 --> 00:03:30.126
it doesn't go down.

00:03:30.126 --> 00:03:33.254
Most of the time, if they
have walls like that,

00:03:33.254 --> 00:03:36.549
it goes down when they are closed,

00:03:36.549 --> 00:03:39.510
and they connect them to
the ground or to the wall.

00:03:39.510 --> 00:03:41.179
They don't open up anymore.

00:03:41.179 --> 00:03:42.972
But it doesn't come all of a sudden

00:03:42.972 --> 00:03:44.766
when an alarm goes off

00:03:44.766 --> 00:03:46.684
because a painting comes off the wall.

00:03:53.775 --> 00:03:56.986
My God, you know, you can't make this up.

00:03:56.986 --> 00:03:58.404
Well apparently you can.

00:03:58.404 --> 00:04:01.950
He takes an oil painting,
and he folds it in two.

00:04:01.950 --> 00:04:03.159
Can you imagine?

00:04:04.202 --> 00:04:07.956
The painting is nailed on a
wooden stretcher, so it breaks.

00:04:07.956 --> 00:04:09.457
The frame, most of the time,

00:04:09.457 --> 00:04:11.334
together, it's with
some, what's it called?

00:04:11.334 --> 00:04:13.211
Plexiglass, you know?

00:04:13.211 --> 00:04:14.254
So you won't get it out,

00:04:14.254 --> 00:04:15.630
because it's connected to each other.

00:04:15.630 --> 00:04:17.131
You really have to take some time

00:04:17.131 --> 00:04:19.217
to disconnect it from each other.

00:04:19.217 --> 00:04:21.052
Heists, especially art heists,

00:04:21.052 --> 00:04:23.680
are normally done by a gentleman thief,

00:04:23.680 --> 00:04:25.765
but the reality is different.

00:04:25.765 --> 00:04:28.851
Normally these heists are
linked to organized crime.

00:04:28.851 --> 00:04:30.561
I have to deal with mobsters,

00:04:30.561 --> 00:04:32.397
with narcos, with terrorists.

00:04:32.397 --> 00:04:33.856
To give you one example,

00:04:33.856 --> 00:04:37.110
the second most searched painting,

00:04:37.110 --> 00:04:39.904
second on the FBI most wanted
list is the Caravaggio.

00:04:39.904 --> 00:04:43.866
It was stolen in 1969, in Palermo, Sicily.

00:04:43.866 --> 00:04:46.494
And it's said that it's being displayed

00:04:46.494 --> 00:04:49.706
at meeting between mafia bosses.

00:04:49.706 --> 00:04:53.876
So that's the kind of
people we have to deal with.

00:04:56.963 --> 00:04:59.257
Most of the time, when
it comes to burglaries,

00:04:59.257 --> 00:05:02.093
it's human error, because you must imagine

00:05:02.093 --> 00:05:04.387
if you're in a museum,
nothing ever happens.

00:05:04.387 --> 00:05:07.140
So if something happens, everybody panics,

00:05:07.140 --> 00:05:08.766
and they don't know,
they forgot the rules.

00:05:08.766 --> 00:05:10.184
They get mixed up.

00:05:10.184 --> 00:05:11.019
I rate

00:05:11.019 --> 00:05:12.145
a three.

00:05:12.145 --> 00:05:14.147
Geez, that's generous.
Or a two.

00:05:14.147 --> 00:05:15.273
A two.

00:05:15.273 --> 00:05:18.901
Every transaction meticulously recorded

00:05:18.901 --> 00:05:21.654
for posterity in the family ledger.

00:05:21.654 --> 00:05:23.531
You see two paintings that were stolen,

00:05:23.531 --> 00:05:25.658
but they were not in the movie.

00:05:25.658 --> 00:05:28.494
They were at my friend's house.

00:05:28.494 --> 00:05:32.206
You see the two paintings
that made Octave famous.

00:05:32.206 --> 00:05:34.709
The two Van Goghs that he stole in 2002.

00:05:34.709 --> 00:05:38.421
But in reality, they were
not there on their couch,

00:05:38.421 --> 00:05:40.256
but it was under his bed.

00:05:40.256 --> 00:05:43.760
He sold it to a mafia boss in Italy,

00:05:43.760 --> 00:05:47.638
who later used these paintings
to get a lesser sentence.

00:05:47.638 --> 00:05:51.225
So luckily, they came back in one piece.

00:05:51.225 --> 00:05:54.645
And some of the time, you have people

00:05:54.645 --> 00:05:56.272
who steal it and they figure out

00:05:56.272 --> 00:05:58.566
that they can't sell it,
and they panic, right?

00:05:58.566 --> 00:05:59.776
And they destroy it.

00:05:59.776 --> 00:06:01.944
When this happened in the
series "The Blacklist,"

00:06:01.944 --> 00:06:03.529
my telephone crashed.

00:06:03.529 --> 00:06:05.531
I was after him, and
now that he has stopped

00:06:05.531 --> 00:06:07.700
doing these things, we
are working together,

00:06:07.700 --> 00:06:09.660
trying to get these things

00:06:09.660 --> 00:06:11.162
prevented from happening, you know.

00:06:11.162 --> 00:06:12.622
Arthur has his contacts.

00:06:12.622 --> 00:06:14.165
He's got some information.

00:06:14.165 --> 00:06:15.375
He doesn't tell me everything.

00:06:15.375 --> 00:06:17.585
I go out on the streets,
talk to my people.

00:06:17.585 --> 00:06:19.253
They don't talk to him.

00:06:19.253 --> 00:06:21.130
And in the middle, we figure things

00:06:21.130 --> 00:06:23.216
that other people can solve.

00:06:23.216 --> 00:06:24.926
This is your chance to solve

00:06:24.926 --> 00:06:29.931
a century and a half's worth
of abductions and thefts.

00:06:30.181 --> 00:06:34.519
So for me, it was something
that I can make some money.

00:06:34.519 --> 00:06:37.063
I never knew that people got hurt,

00:06:37.063 --> 00:06:39.148
if paintings are stolen.

00:06:39.148 --> 00:06:40.817
I didn't understand at the time.

00:06:40.817 --> 00:06:41.818
I do right now

00:06:41.818 --> 00:06:43.861
understand that people get hurt

00:06:43.861 --> 00:06:45.238
if paintings are stolen,

00:06:45.238 --> 00:06:48.199
and that's one of the reasons
I get some paintings back now.

00:06:48.199 --> 00:06:50.243
10 out of 10.

00:06:50.243 --> 00:06:51.494
Tell me about this one.

00:06:52.995 --> 00:06:55.873
Also from Benin, seventh century.

00:06:55.873 --> 00:06:57.667
Fula tribe, I believe.

00:06:58.751 --> 00:07:00.128
Nah.

00:07:00.128 --> 00:07:02.130
It was taken by British soldiers in Benin,

00:07:02.130 --> 00:07:04.132
but it's from Wakanda.

00:07:04.132 --> 00:07:06.008
She's like the director of the museum.

00:07:06.008 --> 00:07:08.469
And nobody approaches, the director

00:07:08.469 --> 00:07:11.013
doesn't approach, or anyone, a guy.

00:07:11.013 --> 00:07:14.267
Art stolen from Africa
is a hot item these days.

00:07:14.267 --> 00:07:16.060
Countries from Africa are demanding

00:07:16.060 --> 00:07:17.979
these pieces back from Western museums,

00:07:17.979 --> 00:07:20.273
because they were stolen
during the colonial era.

00:07:20.273 --> 00:07:22.942
And many of these objects do have

00:07:22.942 --> 00:07:25.027
a spiritual meaning for these countries.

00:07:25.027 --> 00:07:27.196
And in fact, I was one of the first,

00:07:27.196 --> 00:07:30.658
last year, who brought
one of such pieces back.

00:07:30.658 --> 00:07:33.035
An empress crown stolen from Ethiopia,

00:07:33.035 --> 00:07:35.246
and we brought it back, and we handed over

00:07:35.246 --> 00:07:36.998
to the prime minister in Ethiopia.

00:07:38.916 --> 00:07:40.668
I'm going to take a break.

00:07:40.668 --> 00:07:41.961
It is an inside job, you know.

00:07:41.961 --> 00:07:45.756
There is somebody in it,
in a bank, or in a museum,

00:07:45.756 --> 00:07:48.968
who knows about what's
going to take place.

00:07:48.968 --> 00:07:50.761
It's very easy to get somebody inside.

00:07:50.761 --> 00:07:52.054
It's very easy.

00:07:52.054 --> 00:07:53.097
I did these things, you know.

00:07:53.097 --> 00:07:55.224
You have to find somebody
who has problems,

00:07:55.224 --> 00:07:57.101
you know, has debt or something.

00:07:57.101 --> 00:07:58.519
I traced them, where they go,

00:07:58.519 --> 00:08:01.481
where they go out, and
then I bump up to them

00:08:01.481 --> 00:08:02.315
saying, "I'm sorry.

00:08:02.315 --> 00:08:03.441
You want something to drink?"

00:08:03.441 --> 00:08:06.569
But also a strange thing, they
come with their open face.

00:08:06.569 --> 00:08:07.737
He wants to be noticed.

00:08:07.737 --> 00:08:09.363
He attracts attention.

00:08:09.363 --> 00:08:11.032
Yeah, it's too much.

00:08:15.036 --> 00:08:16.162
That's just a taste.

00:08:18.706 --> 00:08:20.500
The problem with museums is that

00:08:20.500 --> 00:08:22.251
they are there for the public.

00:08:22.251 --> 00:08:24.712
They are not, it's not
meant to be a fortress.

00:08:24.712 --> 00:08:27.465
So it's very hard to really prevent

00:08:27.465 --> 00:08:29.884
everybody from taking something.

00:08:29.884 --> 00:08:31.010
You cannot put every painting,

00:08:31.010 --> 00:08:34.680
like the Mona Lisa,
behind such thick glass.

00:08:37.725 --> 00:08:39.519
I've never heard of an art theft

00:08:39.519 --> 00:08:41.771
where people were killed, you know.

00:08:41.771 --> 00:08:44.982
They shoot people here like
it's, you know, it's war.

00:08:44.982 --> 00:08:45.816
It's not like that.

00:08:45.816 --> 00:08:49.570
Let's say you have stolen
art from a crime like this.

00:08:49.570 --> 00:08:50.404
You cannot sell it.

00:08:50.404 --> 00:08:51.239
It's too hard.

00:08:51.239 --> 00:08:53.115
People died, you cannot sell it.

00:08:53.115 --> 00:08:54.075
Ridiculous claim.

00:08:58.621 --> 00:08:59.956
This is a mission impossible.

00:08:59.956 --> 00:09:02.291
It's really a mission impossible.

00:09:02.291 --> 00:09:06.003
It's too much of a risk
to abseil a guy down,

00:09:06.003 --> 00:09:09.090
with a guy just holding a rope.

00:09:09.090 --> 00:09:12.093
If it goes through his
hands, he would drop dead.

00:09:12.093 --> 00:09:13.177
He has no control.

00:09:13.177 --> 00:09:14.220
This is ...

00:09:17.223 --> 00:09:19.267
I cannot even imagine doing this.

00:09:19.267 --> 00:09:20.309
He's half naked.

00:09:20.309 --> 00:09:22.103
There was one case I know of that

00:09:22.103 --> 00:09:23.396
they did this abseiling.

00:09:23.396 --> 00:09:27.108
It was a couple of years ago
in England, in a warehouse.

00:09:27.108 --> 00:09:29.610
They abseiled into this warehouse,

00:09:29.610 --> 00:09:33.364
and the robbery took five hours,

00:09:33.364 --> 00:09:35.825
and they stole 200 rare books,

00:09:35.825 --> 00:09:38.953
amongst them first editions of Galileo,

00:09:38.953 --> 00:09:41.581
for example, worth $3 million.

00:09:41.581 --> 00:09:44.959
He hangs on top of the, what is it?

00:09:44.959 --> 00:09:49.297
A case, and he's sweaty as hell.

00:09:49.297 --> 00:09:51.215
But at that time, they didn't have DNA,

00:09:51.215 --> 00:09:54.135
so it was possible at the
time, but now if you do that,

00:09:54.135 --> 00:09:56.804
they come and get you
after, I think, two weeks.

00:09:56.804 --> 00:09:58.055
They will be at your house.

00:09:58.055 --> 00:09:59.682
In real, it's very heavy.

00:09:59.682 --> 00:10:01.183
I think it's even impossible to lift it up

00:10:01.183 --> 00:10:02.310
the way they do it.

00:10:02.310 --> 00:10:05.354
You know, you need sort of hydraulics,

00:10:05.354 --> 00:10:06.606
you know, to do this.

00:10:13.946 --> 00:10:15.656
This replacing is interesting,

00:10:15.656 --> 00:10:17.116
because it does happen.

00:10:17.116 --> 00:10:20.703
The most spectacular case was
20 years ago in Venezuela.

00:10:21.871 --> 00:10:24.707
A guy was walking around,
visiting art galleries,

00:10:24.707 --> 00:10:27.376
and he wanted to sell them a painting

00:10:27.376 --> 00:10:30.671
called "Odalisque in Red
Pants," made by Henri Matisse.

00:10:30.671 --> 00:10:32.673
But everybody laughed
him away, and they said,

00:10:32.673 --> 00:10:35.092
look, this is a fake, because the real one

00:10:35.092 --> 00:10:37.303
is in a museum in Venezuela.

00:10:37.303 --> 00:10:39.972
So everybody laughed and got him out.

00:10:39.972 --> 00:10:42.058
But there was one art
dealer who got suspicious,

00:10:42.058 --> 00:10:43.809
so he called the museum and he said,

00:10:43.809 --> 00:10:45.978
"Is this painting still in your museum?"

00:10:45.978 --> 00:10:47.104
And they looked at the painting

00:10:47.104 --> 00:10:48.939
and then they found out that the painting

00:10:48.939 --> 00:10:51.442
in the museum was a forgery.

00:10:51.442 --> 00:10:56.447
So somehow, someone had changed
the real one for a fake one.

00:10:57.365 --> 00:10:58.282
Can you imagine you think you have

00:10:58.282 --> 00:10:59.784
two real paintings, two real Van Goghs,

00:10:59.784 --> 00:11:02.995
and you sell them to a heavy guy,

00:11:02.995 --> 00:11:06.707
and he finds out it's fake?

00:11:06.707 --> 00:11:08.668
You're dead.

00:11:08.668 --> 00:11:10.211
Things like this have happened,

00:11:10.211 --> 00:11:12.963
so let's give it, you
know, I give it a seven.

00:11:12.963 --> 00:11:13.923
What do you give it?

00:11:13.923 --> 00:11:14.840
I give it a four.

00:11:14.840 --> 00:11:15.966
It's not that bad.

00:11:15.966 --> 00:11:16.801
Yeah.

00:11:17.968 --> 00:11:19.470
It's fun to watch.

00:11:27.561 --> 00:11:29.271
You never go to your own house

00:11:29.271 --> 00:11:30.940
after you just stole the painting,

00:11:30.940 --> 00:11:34.318
and him touching everything
with his bare hands.

00:11:34.318 --> 00:11:35.736
He doesn't have a hand glove on.

00:11:35.736 --> 00:11:36.696
You never touch the painting

00:11:36.696 --> 00:11:40.074
with your bare hands, because
of the DNA, fingerprints.

00:11:40.074 --> 00:11:42.410
Couple of years ago,
they found a stolen Degas

00:11:42.410 --> 00:11:44.328
in a suitcase in a bus,

00:11:44.328 --> 00:11:46.205
so the police asked all the passengers,

00:11:46.205 --> 00:11:47.957
but nobody claimed the
painting, of course.

00:11:47.957 --> 00:11:50.835
So it does happen that
they put it in suitcases.

00:11:50.835 --> 00:11:54.004
Most paintings I've seen
have always been in a bag,

00:11:54.004 --> 00:11:56.507
trash bag or something,
a trash can bag, right?

00:11:56.507 --> 00:11:58.300
Or a sport bag.

00:11:58.300 --> 00:12:00.094
You got a warrant?

00:12:00.094 --> 00:12:01.721
I don't need a warrant.

00:12:01.721 --> 00:12:05.182
I'm not a cop. But these guys are.

00:12:05.182 --> 00:12:08.060
The mistake that's often
made in Hollywood movies,

00:12:08.060 --> 00:12:12.648
Interpol does not have agents
in the field making arrests.

00:12:12.648 --> 00:12:16.485
Interpol is an agency
that collects information

00:12:16.485 --> 00:12:18.279
and distributes it around the world.

00:12:18.279 --> 00:12:19.989
It's very important work,

00:12:19.989 --> 00:12:23.367
but these men and women
are sitting behind a desk.

00:12:23.367 --> 00:12:25.995
But over and over again,
Hollywood takes them

00:12:25.995 --> 00:12:28.205
from their desk, gives them a gun,

00:12:28.205 --> 00:12:29.290
and lets them make arrests.

00:12:29.290 --> 00:12:31.667
So this just doesn't happen.

00:12:31.667 --> 00:12:34.712
I know it's true, because I
got extradited by Interpol.

00:12:34.712 --> 00:12:36.338
He was talking to the Spanish cops,

00:12:36.338 --> 00:12:38.674
and he never said a word
to me, and he went away.

00:12:38.674 --> 00:12:41.135
He just opened the doors at the airport,

00:12:41.135 --> 00:12:42.845
put me into a plane.

00:12:42.845 --> 00:12:44.305
I love all the little dots.

00:12:45.639 --> 00:12:46.932
It's very artistic.

00:12:47.933 --> 00:12:50.644
There are not many art
detectives in the world,

00:12:50.644 --> 00:12:53.481
you know, police officers who
dedicate themselves to art.

00:12:53.481 --> 00:12:54.648
There are a few.

00:12:54.648 --> 00:12:57.026
Some of them do have knowledge of art,

00:12:57.026 --> 00:13:00.279
but some of them have no clue.

00:13:00.279 --> 00:13:03.824
But let's be honest, the way
he handles this painting,

00:13:03.824 --> 00:13:05.910
it's like they wouldn't allow him

00:13:05.910 --> 00:13:07.411
to touch the paint like that.

00:13:07.411 --> 00:13:10.498
I would say a five, six.

00:13:10.498 --> 00:13:11.332
A three.

00:13:14.585 --> 00:13:16.337
They're all back on.

00:13:16.337 --> 00:13:18.756
This is unrealistic, really.

00:13:18.756 --> 00:13:23.719
And I never heard of a
CCTV camera being override.

00:13:23.719 --> 00:13:25.805
I think it might have happened, you know,

00:13:25.805 --> 00:13:27.515
especially Secret Service has probably

00:13:27.515 --> 00:13:28.641
used this kind of stuff,

00:13:28.641 --> 00:13:32.603
but I don't know of a museum
robbery that this took place.

00:13:32.603 --> 00:13:35.356
If your security system
goes down like an error,

00:13:35.356 --> 00:13:38.359
I'm sure they have regulations or protocol

00:13:38.359 --> 00:13:40.194
what they have to do, check everything.

00:13:40.194 --> 00:13:42.947
You have cameras, obvious and
hidden cameras, of course.

00:13:42.947 --> 00:13:47.159
But what strikes me a lot
is that, in many cases,

00:13:47.159 --> 00:13:49.453
when a theft happens, I
call the police and say,

00:13:49.453 --> 00:13:51.580
"Where is the camera footage?"

00:13:51.580 --> 00:13:55.459
"No, because they were repairing cameras."

00:13:55.459 --> 00:13:57.461
It's like all the cameras in all museums,

00:13:57.461 --> 00:14:01.340
the whole year, they are being repaired.

00:14:01.340 --> 00:14:06.011
♪ He can't write his name or read a book ♪

00:14:06.011 --> 00:14:09.181
Safes hidden behind paintings.

00:14:09.181 --> 00:14:10.432
That does happen.

00:14:10.432 --> 00:14:12.393
But the thieves know that, of course,

00:14:12.393 --> 00:14:14.353
so that's the first place they look.

00:14:14.353 --> 00:14:16.438
And if they don't know,
the moment they take

00:14:16.438 --> 00:14:17.857
a painting off the wall to steal it,

00:14:17.857 --> 00:14:19.316
they see the safe and they think,

00:14:19.316 --> 00:14:20.943
oh my God, we have a bonus.

00:14:20.943 --> 00:14:23.445
That happened with me a couple of times.

00:14:23.445 --> 00:14:24.864
I never was looking for a safe.

00:14:24.864 --> 00:14:28.576
I took a painting, hey, we got a safe.

00:14:28.576 --> 00:14:29.869
You see?

00:14:29.869 --> 00:14:31.453
So don't try this at home.

00:14:31.453 --> 00:14:32.663
Don't do it.

00:14:32.663 --> 00:14:34.832
The door is the most difficult thing

00:14:34.832 --> 00:14:36.417
because it's very thick,

00:14:36.417 --> 00:14:38.335
and the mechanism and stuff like that.

00:14:38.335 --> 00:14:42.548
You always go through the
wall, underneath the safe,

00:14:42.548 --> 00:14:45.092
the back of the safe,
the weak spots, you know?

00:14:45.092 --> 00:14:47.469
♪ If that sort of life is what you wish ♪

00:14:47.469 --> 00:14:50.014
I listened to music when I was on my way

00:14:50.014 --> 00:14:52.933
to get, you know, get some adrenaline.

00:14:52.933 --> 00:14:54.685
I would say like a four.

00:14:54.685 --> 00:14:55.519
Yeah, three or four.

00:14:55.519 --> 00:14:58.188
I don't think you've
been properly introduced.

00:14:59.732 --> 00:15:02.151
Sam, that's your neighbor, Mr. Rembrandt.

00:15:07.448 --> 00:15:09.783
The guy who is standing next to Clooney

00:15:09.783 --> 00:15:11.076
is based on a real character.

00:15:11.076 --> 00:15:12.494
His name is Harry Ettlinger.

00:15:12.494 --> 00:15:15.414
As a little kid, a Jewish kid in Germany,

00:15:15.414 --> 00:15:17.499
he was living in Karlsruhe,

00:15:17.499 --> 00:15:19.376
a few blocks away from the museum

00:15:19.376 --> 00:15:20.628
where this painting was hanging.

00:15:20.628 --> 00:15:22.463
And as a child, he wanted
to see this painting,

00:15:22.463 --> 00:15:23.297
but he was not allowed.

00:15:23.297 --> 00:15:24.298
He was Jewish.

00:15:24.298 --> 00:15:26.884
They didn't allow him into this museum.

00:15:26.884 --> 00:15:30.387
A couple of years later, his family fled

00:15:30.387 --> 00:15:32.014
from the Nazis to the United States.

00:15:32.014 --> 00:15:33.724
And when the war started,

00:15:33.724 --> 00:15:38.479
Harry joined the Army, and this unit,

00:15:38.479 --> 00:15:42.399
they had to track down all the
art stolen by Adolf Hitler.

00:15:42.399 --> 00:15:46.111
They went into a salt mine
in Altaussee, in Austria,

00:15:46.111 --> 00:15:47.488
and Harry, to his surprise,

00:15:47.488 --> 00:15:50.407
saw this same painting, this Rembrandt.

00:15:50.407 --> 00:15:51.700
So I couldn't believe it.

00:15:51.700 --> 00:15:55.537
As an American sergeant,
he brought it back himself.

00:15:55.537 --> 00:15:56.789
What a story.

00:16:04.546 --> 00:16:08.759
Well, the Nazis stole
millions of pieces of art.

00:16:08.759 --> 00:16:11.553
There was given an order
to blow everything up,

00:16:11.553 --> 00:16:13.555
but the second man in command

00:16:13.555 --> 00:16:17.685
from the SS, Kaltenbrunner, prevented it.

00:16:17.685 --> 00:16:20.854
So in the end, this wasn't blown up.

00:16:20.854 --> 00:16:22.690
They found all these pieces back.

00:16:26.652 --> 00:16:28.195
This is an interesting scene.

00:16:28.195 --> 00:16:30.239
Here, you see a painting called

00:16:30.239 --> 00:16:32.950
Portrait of a Young Man, by Raphael.

00:16:32.950 --> 00:16:34.994
And this one is considered to be

00:16:34.994 --> 00:16:36.495
the most important painting

00:16:36.495 --> 00:16:39.665
still missing from World War II, this one.

00:16:39.665 --> 00:16:44.628
But it probably wasn't in
this salt mine to begin with.

00:16:45.504 --> 00:16:48.465
Even I am still searching
for this particular painting.

00:16:48.465 --> 00:16:50.092
The Nazis did destroy art,

00:16:50.092 --> 00:16:51.593
but that was modern art, you know.

00:16:51.593 --> 00:16:53.429
The order was given to destroy them,

00:16:53.429 --> 00:16:55.431
but it didn't take place.

00:16:55.431 --> 00:16:58.851
For reality, I would give it like a nine.

00:17:05.107 --> 00:17:06.734
This painting there,

00:17:06.734 --> 00:17:09.278
the Duke of Wellington by Francisco Goya,

00:17:09.278 --> 00:17:12.614
was really stolen in '61, and in '62,

00:17:12.614 --> 00:17:16.410
when this movie was
shot, it still was lost.

00:17:16.410 --> 00:17:19.038
The maker started to be a little in-joke

00:17:19.038 --> 00:17:20.414
to put it in the movie.

00:17:20.414 --> 00:17:22.374
The real thief was caught

00:17:22.374 --> 00:17:24.293
a few years later, after this movie.

00:17:24.293 --> 00:17:25.669
It was not Dr. No.

00:17:25.669 --> 00:17:28.005
It was a taxi driver from London.

00:17:28.005 --> 00:17:30.424
It influenced a lot of thieves.

00:17:30.424 --> 00:17:32.176
So they steal a painting,

00:17:32.176 --> 00:17:34.636
and then they are going
to search for Dr. No

00:17:34.636 --> 00:17:38.182
to sell it to, but there
are not many Dr. Nos,

00:17:38.182 --> 00:17:40.934
because why would you
buy a stolen painting

00:17:40.934 --> 00:17:42.644
if you have the money to buy a real one.

00:17:42.644 --> 00:17:45.189
You cannot show a stolen
painting to your friends,

00:17:45.189 --> 00:17:46.523
to your children.

00:17:46.523 --> 00:17:50.235
So there are not that many Dr. Nos.

00:17:50.235 --> 00:17:55.199
But the police know, so
they decide to play along.

00:17:55.324 --> 00:17:57.493
So if you meet a Dr. No, most likely,

00:17:57.493 --> 00:18:00.079
it's an undercover cop.

00:18:00.079 --> 00:18:02.873
You know, it's James Bond,
so let's give it a 10.

00:18:02.873 --> 00:18:05.334
I don't watch that many
Hollywood movies, you know,

00:18:05.334 --> 00:18:06.794
because with the kind of work I do,

00:18:06.794 --> 00:18:09.880
sometimes I have the feeling
I'm in one of myself.

00:18:09.880 --> 00:18:14.259
I once recovered a stolen
Picasso worth $70 million.

00:18:14.259 --> 00:18:16.553
But before handing it back to the owner,

00:18:16.553 --> 00:18:18.388
I put it on my wall for one night.

00:18:18.388 --> 00:18:19.973
So imagine, a stolen Picasso worth

00:18:19.973 --> 00:18:22.059
$70 million on my wall.

00:18:22.059 --> 00:18:23.393
There was only one guy in the world

00:18:23.393 --> 00:18:24.853
who knows what it is to have

00:18:24.853 --> 00:18:27.022
these kinds of stone
paintings on his wall.

00:18:27.022 --> 00:18:28.690
So I called Oky, and I said,

00:18:28.690 --> 00:18:30.776
"You have no idea what's on my wall,"

00:18:30.776 --> 00:18:32.361
and he said, "Well, tell me." And I said,

00:18:32.361 --> 00:18:35.322
"A stolen Picasso worth $70 million."

00:18:35.322 --> 00:18:38.867
So he went quiet for a few
seconds and then he said,

00:18:38.867 --> 00:18:41.245
"Is there any chance you're
leaving your home tonight?"

00:18:41.245 --> 00:18:42.371
Thank you for watching.

00:18:42.371 --> 00:18:45.165
If you want to see more,
click the video above.

